{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794052741,"nonce":"W2D3WGvMXSWsJ9VU5Hbtc6Zx6jQ=","message":"First cut landed in the working tree: crates/compiler/src/immix_heap.rs, selected with ZYNTAX_HOST_HEAP=immix. It is a HostHeap table, so the pool and the mark-sweep collector are untouched and both run from the same binary.\n\nDesign as built: one reservation carved into 32 KiB blocks of 128 B lines; start bytes per 16 B quantum (mark bit in the start byte), span lengths per line, a line-mark bitmap per block. Small objects pack at 16 B and may straddle a line (an early version that skipped to the next line charged 1.6x the bytes per object and collected 1.6x as often). Mediums up to 16 KiB own whole lines; larger and Held blocks come from the system allocator. Sweep frees blocks with no marked line and turns runs of unmarked lines into bump regions; reuse zeroes them. Conservative stack, root-range, fiber-window and held-address scanning as the pool collector does. Collections run only on the thread that claimed the heap and stop for good if another thread allocates. Pacing is the pool collector's rule (2x live, 4x after an unproductive collection, 32 MB floor). Knobs: ZYNTAX_IMMIX_HEAP_MB, _FLOOR_KB, _GROWTH, _TRACE.\n\nCorrectness: 7 unit tests; zylua benchmarks give identical output at a 256 KB floor; the Lua conformance suite passes 40 of 46 with the heap selected. The 6 failures are the weak-table, ephemeron and finalizer cases (10 gc/ cases, and the official suite stops at gengc.lua:122): those hooks belong to the pool collector and nothing runs them under a host heap. Next step: implement Marking over this heap and run WeakHooks, the sweepers and after-collection callbacks.\n\nWall time and peak RSS, min/median/max, 5 to 10 interleaved runs. pool is the default; poolgc is the pool with explicit releases ignored (a temporary experiment, since removed), the fair GC-only baseline:\n- tok2 (gmatch count loop): poolgc 2.53 s / 231 MB, immix 2.73 s / 221 MB\n- 3M-entry table build: poolgc 0.29 s / 612 MB, immix 0.31 s / 627 MB\n- hold 1.5M live while churning: poolgc 2.62 s / 1447 MB, immix 2.47 s / 1560 MB\n- churn then build: poolgc 2.51 s / 619 MB, immix 2.91 s / 623 MB\n- pipeline: 0.22 s / 151 MB vs 0.21 s / 156 MB\n- Lua binary trees: 0.11 s / 63 MB vs 0.14 s / 66 MB; depth 18: 2.3 s vs 3.05 s at equal collection counts (61)\n- records 0.06 s vs 0.07 s; tokenize 0.09 s vs 0.10 s; Python binary trees 0.37 s vs 0.45 s\nExplicit releases barely matter for these programs (pool vs poolgc is level), so the gap is the heap, not the release policy.\n\nWhere the gap is: a 41 MB live set marks in about 15 ms against 9.6 ms for the pool collector, and the allocation path adds a thread-local lookup and an out-of-line host call per allocation. Profile on depth-18 binary trees: Marker::consider 28%, sweep and scan 9%, alloc 5.5%, tlv_get_addr 4%.\n\nNot done: pointer-free allocation hint, inline bump for compiled code, prefetching in the mark loop, returning memory to the OS, parallel mark, line-level evacuation, multi-thread stop-the-world.","files":null}]}